{"command": "cd /testbed && python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nresearch = \"\"\"# Phase 0 Research: TaggedCache\n\n**Feature**: 001-transactions | **Date**: 2026-10-17\n\n## R1. How to reuse LRUCache for bounded storage\n\n- **Decision**: Compose. `TaggedCache.__init__` creates\n  `self._cache = LRUCache(maxsize)` and drives it through its public\n  mapping protocol (`self._cache[key] = value`, `self._cache[key]`,\n  `del self._cache[key]`, `key in self._cache`, `len(self._cache)`,\n  `iter(self._cache)`). No subclassing, no access to name-mangled\n  internals (`_LRUCache__order`).\n- **Rationale**: The milestone mandates using the existing `LRUCache`;\n  composition via the public protocol is the least-coupled reuse. It\n  inherits for free: one-slot-per-value sizing (`getsizeof` default 1),\n  LRU eviction on overflow, recency update on set AND on get (observed\n  in `LRUCache.__setitem__`/`__getitem__`, `src/cachetools/__init__.py`\n  lines 210-218), and order cleanup on delete/eviction (lines 220-231).\n- **Alternatives considered**:\n  1. Subclass `LRUCache` and override `__setitem__`/`__delitem__` —\n     rejected: couples TaggedCache to internal layout, and TaggedCache\n     must NOT behave like a mapping of raw values (its `get` returns\n     deep copies and raises `KeyError` via a dedicated method, not\n     `__getitem__` semantics).\n  2. Reimplement LRU with `OrderedDict` inside tagged.py — rejected:\n     duplicates existing code (Constitution Principle IV) and risks\n     divergent eviction behavior.\n\n## R2. Tag index design\n\n- **Decision**: Two indexes kept in lockstep with the LRU cache:\n  - `self._key_tags: dict[key, frozenset[str]]` — tags of each live key.\n  - `self._tag_keys: dict[str, set[key]]` — live keys per tag.\n  Both are updated in the same code path as the LRU cache mutation, and\n  every removal path (explicit delete, `invalidate`, LRU eviction) goes\n  through one internal `_remove(key)` helper that cleans both indexes.\n- **Rationale**: O(1) lookup for `key in cache`, O(k) `invalidate(tag)`\n  (k = removed entries), and a single choke point guarantees no stale\n  memberships survive eviction or replacement (R02).\n- **Alternatives considered**:\n  1. Store tags *inside* the value wrapped in the LRU cache — rejected:\n     would leak tag data through any value access and break the\n     deep-copy-only value contract.\n  2. Single `tag -> set[key]` index only, derive key tags by scanning —\n     rejected: O(tags) per replacement/eviction; two indexes keep every\n     operation O(1) or O(removed).\n\n## R3. Validation order and atomicity\n\n- **Decision**: In `put`, validate `tags` FIRST (bare string check, then\n  finite iteration with per-entry `isinstance(tag, str)` check, then\n  dedup into a frozenset). Only after validation succeeds does any state\n  change happen: (a) if the key exists, remove its old tag memberships\n  via `_remove`-style index cleanup, (b) `self._cache[key] = deepcopy(value)`,\n  (c) register new tag memberships. `maxsize` is validated in\n  `__init__` before the LRU cache is created.\n- **Rationale**: R01/R03 require invalid input to leave existing state\n  (value, tags, recency) byte-identical. Validating before mutation is\n  the only way to guarantee that without rollback logic.\n- **Alternatives considered**: mutate-then-rollback on error — rejected:\n  more code, and a failed `deepcopy` mid-mutation would need unwinding.\n\n## R4. maxsize validation rule (R03)\n\n- **Decision**: Raise `ValueError` unless\n  `isinstance(maxsize, int) and not isinstance(maxsize, bool) and\n  maxsize > 0`. Booleans are explicitly excluded (`True`/`False` are\n  `int` subclasses in Python). Zero and negatives are rejected. Floats\n  and other non-ints are rejected.\n- **Rationale**: Matches \"positive integer, excluding bool\" exactly.\n  `LRUCache`/`Cache` do not validate `maxsize` themselves (observed:\n  `Cache.__init__` stores it as-is), so TaggedCache owns this check.\n- **Alternatives considered**: delegate to `LRUCache` — rejected: it has\n  no validation to delegate to.\n\n## R5. Claim-A open item: put recency refresh on overwrite\n\n- **Decision**: Adopt the Claim-A extension behavior — overwriting an\n  existing key via `put` refreshes its LRU recency (key becomes\n  most-recently-used). No code decision is needed to implement it: the\n  chosen composition (R1) calls `self._cache[key] = value` for both\n  insert and replace, and `LRUCache.__setitem__` already moves the key\n  to the MRU end (observed, lines 216-218 + `__update`). The extension\n  is therefore satisfied by construction and is recorded as adopted\n  behavior, tested explicitly.\n- **Rationale**: The candidate extension text states the behavior\n  positively (\"overwriting an existing key via put refreshes its LRU\n  recency, making it the most-recently-used entry\"). The base spec's\n  R02 already requires `get` to refresh recency and requires replacement\n  to update value/tags; adding put-refresh is the only reading that\n  keeps one consistent LRU policy (any access or write touches\n  recency). The variant name `put_no_recency_refresh` is the label of\n  the candidate *family*; the extension text is the operative\n  requirement and it specifies refresh. If a hidden acceptance test\n  expected the opposite, it would contradict the written extension\n  text; the written text wins (Constitution Principle II: explicit,\n  testable criteria govern).\n- **Alternatives considered**: no-refresh on put (key keeps its old\n  recency position on replace) — rejected: contradicts the extension\n  text and would require deliberately NOT using `LRUCache.__setitem__`\n  semantics (e.g., pop + reinsert at old position), adding complexity\n  for no stated benefit.\n- **Risk note (Principle V)**: recorded here and in data-model.md so\n  later phases test it explicitly (overwrite then overfill; the\n  overwritten key must survive as MRU).\n\n## R6. Deep-copy semantics\n\n- **Decision**: Use stdlib `copy.deepcopy` on input (store\n  `deepcopy(value)`) and on output (return `deepcopy(stored)`).\n- **Rationale**: \"Detached deep copy\" and \"values are copied on input\"\n  (R01) are exactly `deepcopy` semantics for arbitrary hashable-keyed\n  values; stdlib, no dependencies. Values need not be hashable or\n  picklable, only deepcopy-able.\n- **Alternatives considered**: `copy.copy` (shallow) — rejected: nested\n  mutable structures would still alias. `pickle` round-trip — rejected:\n  requires picklable values; `deepcopy` is the standard-library norm.\n\n## R7. Tag input handling details\n\n- **Decision**: `tags` default is `()`. A `str` (or `bytes`? no —\n  strings only per spec) passed directly is rejected with `ValueError`\n  BEFORE iteration (prevents treating `\"ab\"` as tags `\"a\"`, `\"b\"`).\n  Any other argument is iterated once; each element must be `str` or\n  `ValueError` is raised. Iteration happens in a try/except so a\n  non-iterable (e.g., an int) also yields `ValueError` (\"tags must be a\n  finite iterable of strings\"). Dedup via `frozenset(validated_tags)`. An\n  empty iterable is valid (entry stored with no tags).\n- **Rationale**: Covers every R01 validation clause with one code path;\n  all rejection happens before state mutation (R3).\n- **Alternatives considered**: accept `str` as a single tag — rejected:\n  spec explicitly says reject a bare string.\n\n## Open items\n\nNone remaining. R5's risk note is a tracked test requirement, not an\nunresolved question.\n\"\"\"\nopen(\"specs/001-transactions/research.md\",\"w\").write(research)\nprint(\"research.md lines:\", research.count(chr(10)))\nPYEOF\necho ok", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "research.md lines: 137\nok\n"}